@Y50000 just to confirm the answer than @ia-davidpichler gave you: each pool can make blocks, with the probability of each pool making a block within a given time being proportional to their stake. The aggregate rate of block production from all pools in the network could be quite high (i.e. blocks produced frequently).

This is exactly the same principle as in the current Cardano (with Praos): the chance of a pool making a block is proportional to the pool stake, and overall the pools make a block on average every 20 seconds. The difference is the rate of block production could be much higher (and the structure of the chain and the algorithm are different) but the block production is still spread out over all pools in a stake-weighted way.

