@KtorZ well, the idea is, currently `enable` returns an object that has all the methods we might use to start interacting with the wallet; if we throw an error, we're interrupting the return of that object.

The idea behind my suggestion is to *still return the same object* from enable; if I absolutely require some feature that isn't in `(await cardano.{wallet}.enable()).extensions`, then I can treat that as an error and show the user some kind of explanatory dialog.  But, if it was just a nice to have, I can just change the behavior of my application to omit that missing feature.

If, instead, we threw an error that just described "here are the features we could turn on if you wanted, and here are those we couldn't", then I have to call enable again with a restricted set, which is going to (potentially) prompt the user again, etc.

