@realdecimalist I've hidden your post above - a single dump of an AI critical summary of the CIP, apparently - since this posting and others like it will have no place in CIP reviews: while on the other hand we might permit smaller quotations supporting a unique conclusion about a complicated subject that could be considered essential to all human readers (though it would still be a human decision whether or not these things were likely).

We need critical thinking and human experience, not linguistic summaries, and we definitely don't need conditioning from 100 different AIs in the global ecosystem each having a go at hundreds of reviewable CIPs in the next few years... so pending consensus from other editors we're trimming that branch of the tree preemptively.

The label of "Spam" is from the technically irrelevant mention of Talos as the review provider.  Note, functionally and rationally speaking, it's AIs that are supposed to act "on behalf of" humans: not the other way around.

**p.s.** the main reasons we don't put those implications in the titles are:
- _brevity_ - there are generally so many implications it could really stuff up the word count & these are by nature defined (and required) in the CIP document itself;
- _subjectivity_ - not only is it subjective which implications would justify space in the title (as being important enough to imply the reader might not see it from reading the document), it's often subjective (or uncertain) about whether such "benefits" are actually achieved.  See all our early CIPs about PoS reward sharing schemes as cases in point.

