@kingslavcho, thank you for continuing to engage with the concerns and for moving from an absolute `MUST support every registered provider` position toward allowing exclusions.

@rphair, your earlier point that CPS-0032 should not be framed as having only one possible solution now appears especially relevant.

After considering the latest response, I believe the remaining disagreement is no longer an editorial detail. It is a foundational architectural difference, and I do not think it is responsible for me to continue appearing as a co-author of a proposal whose central trust model I do not support.

Before addressing authorship, I want to explain the remaining disagreement clearly.

## The proposed compromise still makes registration a presumptive entitlement

The proposed compromise is:

> Wallets implementing this CIP SHOULD resolve all active registry entries meeting the technical criteria. Wallets MAY exclude a provider but MUST publicly document the reason for exclusion.

This is an improvement over an unconditional `MUST`, but it retains the same underlying presumption - a registry entry creates a presumptive right to participate in wallet address resolution, and the wallet bears the burden of publicly defending any refusal.

I do not agree with that model.

Registry conformance should demonstrate that a provider has correctly described its system and passed objective technical tests. It should not create an entitlement to:

- wallet enablement;
- automatic queries;
- user-facing presentation;
- preferred placement;
- address-resolution participation;
- or public justification when a wallet declines support.

## Security decisions cannot require confirmed harm

The proposed valid reasons for exclusion include confirmed security incidents, confirmed fraud, or regulatory requirements.

That threshold is too narrow and, in security terms, often too late.

Wallets must sometimes act before an incident is conclusively proven. Relevant circumstances may include:

- a credible but non-public security disclosure;
- evidence of key or infrastructure compromise;
- suspicious resolver behavior;
- unresolved ownership or control disputes;
- unsafe normalization or collision behavior;
- deficient incident-response capability;
- persistent operational failures;
- privacy or tracking concerns;
- technically valid but misleading resolution semantics;
- inability to support the provider safely within the wallet’s architecture;
- legal advice that cannot be published;
- confidential threat intelligence;
- abusive or bad-faith conduct known to the wallet team.

Requiring a wallet to wait for “confirmed fraud” before disabling a funds-routing dependency is not a safe standard.

Requiring a public explanation can also force disclosure of:

- an active security investigation;
- confidential vulnerability reports;
- private legal advice;
- accusations that create unnecessary legal or defamation exposure.

A wallet may voluntarily publish its provider-support policies and non-sensitive reasons for exclusion. That can be encouraged. It should not be a condition of technical conformance.

A cleaner alternative would be:

> Wallets SHOULD publish general provider-evaluation and support policies.

> Wallets MAY publish provider-specific status and reason codes when doing so does not compromise security, confidentiality, legal obligations, or active investigations.

> Wallets retain discretion to disable or decline any provider or resolution method based on their security, privacy, operational, legal, technical, or user-protection requirements.

That creates transparency without forcing unsafe disclosure.

## Greater visibility does not justify compulsory interaction

I agree that a public registry can make providers more visible. That is a benefit. But visibility and interaction are different things. A bad actor operating outside the registry may be less visible. A bad actor inside a registry becomes more dangerous only if registry inclusion compels wallets to interact with it. The registry should improve discovery and evaluation without automatically crossing the wallet’s trust boundary.

## CIP-30 is not a precedent for compulsory counterparties

CIP-30 standardizes the interface between dApps and wallets. It does not require every CIP-30 wallet to trust or connect to every dApp. That is the relevant distinction.

A technical standard defines what behavior means **when an implementation chooses to support and enable it**. It does not normally require an implementation to establish a trust relationship with every third party that conforms to a schema.

This CIP does not need to compel support for every registered provider.

## Universal compatibility cannot be promised through a registry

The example of a user minting an identifier and later discovering that only two of five wallets support it is framed as the ecosystem misleading that user. The party making the product claim is responsible for describing compatibility accurately. A provider should not claim that an identifier works in every wallet unless every wallet has actually implemented and enabled it.

Declaring universal support normative does not make universal support real. It only makes implementations that exercise independent judgment appear non-conforming.

## ADA Handle moving toward a protocol standard

This is something we have always wanted to do. Evidence of that is throughout our Discord history and probably in our early tweets. It is also in our contracts as they already support the idea of multiple Handle providers. We got caught up in feature development and though we started to create the DAO we believed would be necessary, the latest market conditions caused us to abandon it. We thought a treasury (funded by a portion of Handle sales), voting over contract setting UTxOs, and direction decisions would be a requirement - but maybe we can move forward with a standard without all of that.

**This isn't set in stone, but this would be the general direction:**

Our goal would be to open up the current decentralized minting contracts so anyone can mint a free "basic" SubHandle without our signature (or "near-free" -  to support a treasury). We'd probably use the `$ada` root Handle (i.e. `<your-name>@ada`). 

Since it costs money to render, pin, and host a separate Handle image - basic SubHandles would all have the same empty Handle image as their main IPFS NFT image. Implementors would only need to put the Handle name on top of it to display it. 

Since it is a "basic" SubHandle - the CIP-68 extra field would not be editable and they could not be paired with other CIP-67 tokens in the Handles space.

Registered Handle service providers (Kora Labs being the first and currently only one) could offer to upgrade your Handle - enabling CIP-68 extra features and CIP-67 pairing for extended functionality to support features like on-chain OAuth, Personalization, Portals, Chat, Secrets, Public Datum and more (some are already working, some are WIP).

Current or future paid root Handle owners could also offer free or paid mints of their SubHandles - enabling each root Handle owner to build their own Handle business. This is essentially the namespacing support that prompted much of CPS-0032 and an avenue for @kingslavcho if he wanted instant wallet adoption on an already proven protocol.

This ensures that the Handles ecosystem has a funding mechanism for commercial growth.

## Withdrawal as co-author

Because the present CIP and the alternative I intend to propose use materially different trust and interoperability models, I am withdrawing as a co-author of this CIP.

This is not an attempt to block the proposal.

@kingslavcho should remain free to champion this registry-centered approach on its own technical and philosophical merits. I am also willing to continue participating as a stakeholder and reviewer where useful.

I request the following housekeeping changes:

1. Remove my name from the CIP `Authors` field.
2. Remove “Co-authored with @papag00se (ADA Handle / Kora Labs)” from the PR description.
3. Do not represent the current draft as endorsed by ADA Handle or Kora Labs.
4. Preserve the discussion history and, if appropriate, acknowledge my early participation and review.
5. Once the alternative proposal is submitted, list both proposals independently under the `Proposed Solutions` field of CPS-0032.

@rphair, this appears consistent with your earlier concern that the CPS and CIP titles should not suggest there is only one acceptable solution to the problem.

Two clearly authored proposals are healthier than one nominally co-authored proposal that conceals a fundamental lack of agreement.

## Final distinction

I support:

- open technical standards;
- permissionless innovation;
- reusable resolution profiles;
- transparent provider descriptions;
- conformance testing;
- explicit provider context;
- and safer wallet UX.

I do not support:

- registry inclusion creating presumptive wallet enablement;
- wallet security decisions requiring public defense;
- CIP editors or registry maintainers becoming indirect trust governors;
- or technical conformance being treated as a right to participate in every wallet’s funds-routing path.

A CIP can standardize interoperable behavior. It cannot and should not attempt to transfer an independent wallet’s trust and security decisions to a registry.

